WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.970
FELIPE HOFFA: With over
500 sessions at Next '19,

00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:05.980
it's understandable that you
might have not seen them all.

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:09.190
So if you missed Modern Data
Warehouse with BigQuery,

00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:12.150
where the BigQuery lead
Jordan Tigani shares how

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:14.460
we are transforming
BigQuery into the data

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:16.800
warehouse of the
future, don't worry,

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:18.315
because this is a rewind.

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:26.660
You might already know that
BigQuery is our enterprise data

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:28.680
warehouse built for the cloud.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:31.050
It can easily store
exabyte of data,

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:34.310
as well as run frequent
queries over petabytes of it.

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:38.255
Everything is encrypted,
durable, and highly available.

00:00:38.255 --> 00:00:39.880
And these are some
of the features that

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:42.050
make BigQuery really unique.

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:44.180
It's fully managed
and serverless.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:47.780
It handles streaming data to
provide real time analytics.

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:49.790
It has built in
support for machine

00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:52.190
learning and G analysis.

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:55.160
And high speed
in-memory BI engine

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:59.300
was just added for instant
interactive dashboards.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:01.190
And of course,
during the talk we

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:03.830
had quotes from industry
analysts and customers

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:07.640
that can attest to all of
this, as well as petabyte scale

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:09.320
interactive demos.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:12.800
But let's focus on the
main message of this talk.

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:14.630
How is Google Cloud
putting all of this

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:17.220
together to build
our vision of what

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:21.120
a data warehouse of the
future should look like?

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:22.910
Let's start with the
first two elements.

00:01:22.910 --> 00:01:26.900
A data warehouse should
handle storage and compute.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:29.120
And by that, I mean
the ability to collect

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:32.130
all the data you
need to have together

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:36.510
and the ability to analyze it
and combine it at any scale.

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:40.850
In fact, one of our customers is
storing more than 250 petabytes

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:42.440
of data in BigQuery.

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:45.050
And others have
run a single query

00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:47.630
over more than 5 petabytes.

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:50.570
To pull this off, you really
want your data warehouse

00:01:50.570 --> 00:01:53.150
to be truly serverless.

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:56.120
You get to decide on
your data and the queries

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:59.690
you want to run on it, while we
take care of running everything

00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:01.730
else behind the scenes.

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:04.830
And what's the magic
behind the scenes?

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:07.580
BigQuery's architecture
that separates storage

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:11.540
from compute and a petabyte
network connecting both sides.

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:12.680
Cool!

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:13.990
But that's not enough.

00:02:13.990 --> 00:02:15.920
A modern data
warehouse should be

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:18.050
able to work with
real time data.

00:02:18.050 --> 00:02:21.560
To prove this during the talk,
Jordan ran a dataflow pipeline,

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:25.460
generating data out
of more than 600 CPUs.

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:27.200
As you can see here,
these nodes were

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:30.840
generating more than 2
gigabytes of data per second.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:33.540
And he showed how BigQuery
was able to instantly run

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:38.040
queries and find patterns
over this incoming data.

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:40.820
What else do you expect from
a modern data warehouse?

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:42.870
Centralized storage.

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:44.720
And this is something
new with BigQuery.

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:47.720
We already know that BigQuery
can store all of your data

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:49.080
and analyze it.

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:52.420
But what if we want to
read it from other systems?

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:56.390
BI tools, Batch and Stream
processors, Hadoop, Spark,

00:02:56.390 --> 00:02:58.700
machine learning
platforms, they should all

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:01.190
be able to read data
storage BigQuery.

00:03:01.190 --> 00:03:03.890
And we just announced
that BigQuery stores

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:07.820
API to enable fast parallel
rates without the need

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:09.890
of exporting data first.

00:03:09.890 --> 00:03:10.880
What else?

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:12.740
Security the trust.

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:15.440
To make sure we can put BigQuery
into the hands of our most

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:18.860
sensitive customers, Google has
developed features with them,

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:23.780
like customer managed encryption
keys and access transparency.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:26.510
If anyone managing your
cloud data warehouse

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:28.700
has to look at your
data, you should know

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:30.770
when and why this happened.

00:03:30.770 --> 00:03:32.940
And you get that with BigQuery.

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:35.150
Next, data sharing.

00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:37.790
You want to make BigQuery
available to anyone

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:40.760
that needs to analyze
data in your organization.

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:43.530
On one hand, we have a
strong permission model.

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:45.110
But we also want
to make the power

00:03:45.110 --> 00:03:48.120
of data accessible
to those who need it.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:50.030
So with BigQuery, you
can save and share

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:52.040
queries, as well as data sets.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:55.040
And your analyst can
create beautiful reports

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:57.990
with Data Studio and
third party tools.

00:03:57.990 --> 00:04:01.010
And to make all of these
reports fast and scalable,

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:04.640
BI Engine is a new block that
acts as a transparent catch in

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:08.870
between, an in-memory database
that knows when to bring data

00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:10.760
from the back end,
where you only

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:14.330
need to decide how much
memory you want it to have.

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:17.209
And we're making BigQuery
increasingly accessible,

00:04:17.209 --> 00:04:21.410
even to users that would rather
do everything in a spreadsheet.

00:04:21.410 --> 00:04:24.680
And the last bit of a modern
data warehouse, predictive.

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:28.100
With infinite storage, you can
know what happened in the past.

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:29.930
With real time
streaming data, you

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:31.860
can tell what's happening now.

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:33.485
And with machine
learning capabilities,

00:04:33.485 --> 00:04:36.390
you can predict what
will happen next.

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:38.480
Now BigQuery saved room
to create and run machine

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:41.180
learning models
natively, inside.

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:43.610
Yes, you can create
a model and then

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:46.520
use it to predict,
within BigQuery and only

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:48.830
using a couple SQL commands.

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:52.440
To the initial linear and
logistic regression models,

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:55.190
Google has added [INAUDIBLE]
clustering, and matrix

00:04:55.190 --> 00:04:57.500
factorization, and
even the ability

00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:01.370
to import any TensorFlow model
you created outside BigQuery,

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:03.590
and even trained
TensorFlow models

00:05:03.590 --> 00:05:06.690
without leaving your
favorite data warehouse.

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:10.500
This is our vision of a
modern data warehouse.

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:11.930
But it doesn't stop here.

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:14.540
Take the full talk to see
how one of our customers

00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:16.160
puts everything
together to power

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:18.440
the future, driverless cars.

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:19.620
That's it for now.

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:21.380
If you'd like to
see the full talk,

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:24.010
check out the link in
the description below.

00:05:24.010 --> 00:05:26.720
Now it's your turn
to try BigQuery.

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:30.930
I'm Felipe Hoffa, and
remember to subscribe.

